--- note
## Two frozen metadata containers (lines 124–154)
No image tensor is ever kept in RAM. Instead, two `@dataclass(frozen=True)` records describe *where*
to read from at batch time:
```python
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class FrameWindowData:                 # one window of W consecutive frames (metadata only)
    t_start: int                       # first frame index → read image[t_start : t_start+W]
    n_frames: int                      # W (window size, default 2)
    pos_feats: list[torch.Tensor]      # W tensors, each (N_i, 32) sinusoidal codes
    coords:    list[torch.Tensor]      # W tensors, each (N_i, 3) = z,y,x on the downsampled grid
    node_counts: list[int]             # GT nodes per frame
    targets:   list[torch.Tensor]      # W-1 transition matrices (from tr01)

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class VideoMeta:                       # per-video info enabling on-demand frame loading
    zarr_path: Path                    # the image zarr
    image_shape: tuple                 # (T, Z_ds, Y_ds, X_ds) AFTER downsampling
    downsample: tuple                  # (Z,Y,X) strides applied at I/O time
    voxel_size: tuple                  # physical size = scale × downsample
    q_low: float                       # 0.1% intensity quantile (normalisation)
    q_high: float                      # 99.9% intensity quantile
```
**[Craft]** frozen dataclasses = immutable, hashable, self-documenting. The whole design goal is in
the docstring: *"Workers open the zarr file themselves and load only the two frames they need per
batch item. No full video tensor is kept in RAM during training."*

--- note
### `extract_pos_features` (161–190) — sinusoidal position codes, no intensity
Each node's `(t,z,y,x)` is normalised to `[0,1]` by the image shape, then embedded with a bank of
`2**k · π` frequencies (sin ⊕ cos). With `pos_embed_dim=8` per axis and 4 axes → a **32-dim** code:
```python
def extract_pos_features(coords, image_shape, pos_embed_dim=8):
    t, z, y, x = coords[:,0], coords[:,1], coords[:,2], coords[:,3]   # (N,) each
    norms = [c / max(s, 1) for c, s in zip([t,z,y,x], image_shape)]  # normalise each axis to ~[0,1]
    def _embed(vals):                                                # sinusoidal bank per axis
        freqs = 2 ** np.arange(pos_embed_dim // 2)                   # [1,2,4,8] → 4 freqs
        angles = vals[:, None] * freqs * np.pi                       # (N,4) angles
        return np.concatenate([np.sin(angles), np.cos(angles)], 1)  # (N,8) = sin⊕cos
    return np.concatenate([_embed(n) for n in norms], 1).astype(np.float32)  # (N, 32)
```
Below: run it on **real** frame-0 node coordinates and confirm the `(N, 32)` shape.

--- code
import numpy as np, zarr                                          # arrays + zarr IO
ROOT = "/home/seshu/kaggle/2026/biohub-cell-tracking-during-development"  # project root
TRAIN = f"{ROOT}/input/biohub-cell-tracking-during-development/train"     # labelled embryos
G = f"{TRAIN}/6bba_062c8d37.geff"                                 # one real embryo graph
nt = np.asarray(zarr.open(f"{G}/nodes/props/t/values")[:])       # frame index per node
z  = np.asarray(zarr.open(f"{G}/nodes/props/z/values")[:])       # z per node
y  = np.asarray(zarr.open(f"{G}/nodes/props/y/values")[:])       # y per node
x  = np.asarray(zarr.open(f"{G}/nodes/props/x/values")[:])       # x per node
m0 = nt == 0                                                      # nodes in frame 0
coords = np.column_stack([nt[m0], z[m0], y[m0], x[m0]]).astype(np.float32)  # (N,4) t,z,y,x
image_shape = (100, 200, 2048, 2048)                             # (T,Z,Y,X) for normalisation
def extract_pos_features(coords, image_shape, pos_embed_dim=8):  # the file's exact function
    t,z,y,x = coords[:,0], coords[:,1], coords[:,2], coords[:,3] # split axes
    norms = [c / max(s,1) for c,s in zip([t,z,y,x], image_shape)]# normalise per axis
    def _embed(v):                                               # sinusoidal bank
        f = 2 ** np.arange(pos_embed_dim//2)                     # frequencies [1,2,4,8]
        a = v[:,None]*f*np.pi                                    # angles (N,4)
        return np.concatenate([np.sin(a), np.cos(a)], 1)        # (N,8)
    return np.concatenate([_embed(n) for n in norms], 1).astype(np.float32)  # (N,32)
pf = extract_pos_features(coords, image_shape)                   # real embeddings
{"n_nodes_frame0": coords.shape[0], "pos_feat_dim": pf.shape[1]} # → dim = 4 axes × 8 = 32

